WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.600
WHEN HOLLYWOOD SAYS NO, DON’T LISTEN

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:07.600
Scott Dunn, Writer/Director/Actor/Editor : Hi,
I’m Scott Dunn.

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:08.600
Gina Gomez Dunn, Producer/Actor: And I’m
Gina Gomez Dunn.

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:15.320
Scott: You know, I was thinking about something
today. I was in the kitchen looking at salt,

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:22.180
it made me think about today’s video. I
just thought about the first person...who

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:27.330
is the first person who is like “This, I
should sprinkle this on my food!” I thought

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:31.700
to myself, it was probably a caveman, right?
A caveman?

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:32.700
Gina: Probably.

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:38.579
Scott: [Grunts] Which means this is going
to taste good on my meat. And then I thought

00:00:38.579 --> 00:00:43.920
there was probably another caveman and was
[Grunts]...which is “You can’t do that!

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:47.280
That’s a rock looking thing. You can’t
put that on your food. )

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:51.309
And imagine if the first caveman listened
to that negative caveman, we wouldn't have

00:00:51.309 --> 00:00:54.260
salt all right.

00:00:54.260 --> 00:01:00.149
We wouldn't have salt. Things taste better
with salt.

00:01:00.149 --> 00:01:08.909
Now when you fast-forward to today and 2018
in Hollywood and there's cavemen all over

00:01:08.909 --> 00:01:14.310
Hollywood who keep telling “You can't do
that. You can't do this. You can't do that.”

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:16.700
Gina: A lot of naysayers.

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:21.969
Scott: And we want to talk to you today about
our second feature film MANDAO OF THE DEAD

00:01:21.969 --> 00:01:26.730
and all the things people say you can't do
and we did them anyway and if

00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:29.670
we hadn't we wouldn't be sitting here with
a second feature film

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:31.490
So let's start...

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:42.390
Gina: Number One “You can’t make a movie
(especially a feature film for less than $100K”

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:44.439
or they won't take you seriously

00:01:44.439 --> 00:01:50.909
Well our production budget for MANDAO OF THE
DEAD was just shy of thirteen

00:01:50.909 --> 00:01:57.370
thousand. That's it. I round it up. You know
it's one of those things where you have to

00:01:57.370 --> 00:02:05.719
work with what you got and tailor your script
to fit your budget and tailor your script

00:02:05.719 --> 00:02:11.530
to things that you have available resources
that you have available to you.

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:17.890
Our co-producers Chessa Mehlman and Ian Sumner
graciously lent us their apartment. So we

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:19.280
saved a lot of money on the location.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:24.420
Scott: Say half the movie was shot there so
right? There you know saving a lot of money

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:30.300
when I was writing the script for MANDAO I
made sure that we we stayed within the confines

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:36.160
of I knew how about how much money we had
to spend on this feature film and I thought

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:40.070
“Well I can write it in very few locations
but it doesn't but we didn't sacrifice any

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:44.520
fun.” I think it was about as fun it was
as it was ever gonna be and I actually think

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:47.400
that the limitations made it a better movie.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:53.380
Gina: And even our crew, our sound mixer had
all his own equipment. Our DP had it's all

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:59.780
his own equipment and our associate producer
Oscar Munoz he lent us his camera gear so

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:05.540
it was like just see finding different ways
to save money without sacrificing quality.

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:09.280
Scott: Number two “It can't be done in nine
days? Not a feature film?”

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:15.380
And they were right it was done in ten days
so in that tenth day was really just a pick-up

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:19.450
day a pick-up day with a couple of additional
scenes after editing and I saw that there

00:03:19.450 --> 00:03:23.720
needed to be a few things to fill in and ten
days is still crazy.

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:30.220
But again caveman! You bad caveman! You said
we couldn't do it. No, No!

00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:36.120
Gina: The reason why we were able to do that
is we kept our crew cast and crew really small

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:40.930
so we were able to move fast and we also rehearsed
prior to shooting we've rehearse with an actor.

00:03:40.930 --> 00:03:46.180
Scott: This is something that I'm very gung-ho
about. Always get the rehearsals going before

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:48.830
your movie that's where you know, that's where
you get to work on it because when you're

00:03:48.830 --> 00:03:52.850
shooting you don't have time to explore as
much. You should know what you're doing and

00:03:52.850 --> 00:03:56.200
you know you there's plenty of room for improvisation
on set.

00:03:56.200 --> 00:04:01.180
I'm just saying do a lot of that work in the
rehearsals the third one...this is a personal

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:08.210
experience I had been in contact with a producer
in Hollywood and we were talking and we had

00:04:08.210 --> 00:04:13.290
this sort of disagreement and he said to me
“You're not a writer. You're not a director.

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:17.750
I mean you're barely an actor and I remember
hearing that. It was obviously painful. It’s

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:23.800
painful to be told that you you're not something.
I had just done in the feature film, our first

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:29.020
feature film SCHLEP and it hadn't gotten into
any festivals yet. He's like “What festivals

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:32.900
was it has it gotten into? Yeah that's right,
nothing yet.”

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:37.550
And weirdly enough a week later we got accepted
to our First Glance Film Festival and we had

00:04:37.550 --> 00:04:42.380
a great showing and the audience loved it
and it was just a nice thing where you know

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:46.220
I'm glad we didn't listen to people saying
no. I mean no one knows anything.

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:51.640
Gina: No one can tell you that you can't do
anything. I mean they can say it but don't

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:53.540
believe it for a second.

00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:59.130
Scott: It can be really good. I will say after
all the time I've been in Hollywood it's been

00:04:59.130 --> 00:05:05.630
10 years and I will say this it's about how
you receive words like that. If you people

00:05:05.630 --> 00:05:12.370
say you can't do it, you have really two options:
believe them or show them and I think it was

00:05:12.370 --> 00:05:17.930
actually I'd like to thank that guy because
it pushed me to really prove him wrong.

00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:24.400
Scott: the fourth thing this is an interesting
one I edit my features we've done - I've edited

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:25.400
both.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:33.460
Gina: Okay so so just to recap he Scott wrote
directed starred and now edited MANDAO OF

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:34.460
THE DEAD.

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:39.320
Scott: Gina and I like doing it but also it
saves money. Obviously you know how what our

00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:44.500
budget is it helps save money but I also enjoy
doing it I think that who could know the movie

00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:49.770
better than me at this point but a lot of
people were you know when I was asking on

00:05:49.770 --> 00:05:53.760
SCHLEP like what should I do is SCHLEP was
my first movie so I was like oh should I edit

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:57.070
I get someone else and a lot of people like
you shouldn't edit your own you know you'll

00:05:57.070 --> 00:06:01.730
be too close to it and no it's just not how
it's done and I remember thinking yeah but

00:06:01.730 --> 00:06:06.990
I want to I feel like doing it it's not like
I ever felt burdened by it and it's like who

00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:13.180
knows the movie better than you right exactly
and so going into this MANDAO feature I I

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:19.050
felt galvanized incompetent in editing it
and and I think this is something I'll be

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:24.570
carrying forward to future features because
it's just fine and and you know editing really

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:29.680
it's direct writing is one thing directing
is another thing editing is another thing

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:33.970
like if you can do all that that as a director
the more I think you'll be more enriched as

00:06:33.970 --> 00:06:36.340
an
artist it's also staying true to your own

00:06:36.340 --> 00:06:41.810
voice like how did you envision this movie
well if you're the writer director and editor

00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:43.700
you know you get the last say in everything

00:06:43.700 --> 00:06:49.150
Gina: So it really the film really became
Scott's voice and also they said don't do

00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:54.120
don't use Final Cut Pro I mean everyone's
using Adobe and I again or avid and and that's

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:58.250
true a lot of people are using that and and
there's no problem with that and the only

00:06:58.250 --> 00:07:02.860
problem is when people say you don't use Final
Cut Pro I'm like hi yeah if you can tell me

00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:08.550
why great if not then that kind of information
that it's just so because I don't do it but

00:07:08.550 --> 00:07:10.419
we did it anyway and it turned out great

00:07:10.419 --> 00:07:20.050
Number five oh yeah you can't make money self
distributing your film or in other words you

00:07:20.050 --> 00:07:25.480
need a distributor to make money making your
film that's just not true so for the majority

00:07:25.480 --> 00:07:31.540
people if you're really indie you're making
your first feature it behooves you to self

00:07:31.540 --> 00:07:40.180
distribute because each platform like iTunes
Amazon etc they take a percentage of all the

00:07:40.180 --> 00:07:47.400
sales that you make then the distributor takes
another 30 percent 20 30 whatever yeah after

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:52.150
that it's like then you start seeing some
money and by that point it's like that's a

00:07:52.150 --> 00:07:56.370
long time that's a lot of percentages to be
taking out before you see any money and and

00:07:56.370 --> 00:08:01.460
and in this case of MANDAO OF THE DEAD and
SCHLEP we are putting our own money into it

00:08:01.460 --> 00:08:05.900
we're putting our time sweat equity all these
things into it and it's like why give that

00:08:05.900 --> 00:08:11.200
percentage away especially in a day and age
where you can distribute yourself yeah you

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:17.090
may I think we're trying to out BlumHouse
the BlumHouse model they they make movies

00:08:17.090 --> 00:08:22.990
for very cheap right and then they make between
like they make a ton back I mean the margins

00:08:22.990 --> 00:08:27.620
on their profits are I mean they're it's amazing
so we're doing that but crunching it down

00:08:27.620 --> 00:08:35.339
even further we're talking like $13,000 that's
insane but it also means that you have more

00:08:35.339 --> 00:08:40.259
of a chance of making your money back and
going into black you know I'm making a profit

00:08:40.259 --> 00:08:44.639
with your money and that's me that's attractive
to me so I would suggest let's not let's not

00:08:44.639 --> 00:08:46.920
let people
tell us no when they don't even they haven't

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:51.189
even tried it try it you'll see for yourself

